Postdoc:  ```DIALOGUE
Your proposed additions are excellent and push the boundaries of our initial ideas. I appreciate the direction towards embedding context-specific features to compute difficulty, and the Monte Carlo sampling oracle will add a robust stochastic element to our adaptive selection process. Here’s an updated outline integrating your thoughts:

1. For text generation, we’ll develop a π-learner MDM (scale: 170M–1.1B parameters) that dispenses with time embeddings in favor of a new auxiliary network. This network will derive a dynamic “difficulty score” for each masked token by evaluating its surrounding context. In inference, we’ll extend our adaptive oracle toolkit to include our Monte Carlo oracle (leveraging Gumbel noise) alongside the traditional Top-K probability and margin criteria. Our evaluation will focus on validation loss, generative perplexity (with LLaMA2-7B as an external evaluator), and entropy/diversity metrics.

2. For our synthetic “L&O-NAE-SAT” task, we’ll design a variant featuring long-range dependencies to accentuate subproblem imbalances. We plan to train a smaller MDM (6–19M parameters) and record detailed per-subproblem error metrics. Here, a dual-oracle framework will be implemented—one that uses the classic Top-K margin approach, and another that deploys our Monte Carlo oracle—to directly compare their efficiency in resolving complex interdependent subproblems.

3. For logic puzzles, we will diversify our benchmarks by not only including Sudoku and Zebra but also introducing a grid-based puzzle that mirrors mini-crossword or visual reasoning challenges. We’ll use MDMs scaled to 6–19M parameters for these tasks. Detailed logs will capture the improvement in puzzle solution accuracy per unmasked step, and we’ll compare these results with baseline ARMs (trained with and without teacher-forced ordering) to underline the benefits of adaptive token ordering.

In all experiments, rigorous reproducibility measures will be taken: fixing random seeds, testing over at least three seeds, and logging complete sampling paths along with per-step logits. Our ablation studies will examine variations in oracle type (Top-K vs. margin vs. Monte Carlo), different K values, noise parameters, reverse step counts, and the impact of positional encoding choices (learned vs. RoPE) on the closeness of the learned token order to the identity permutation.

Does this synthesis capture everything we need, or would you suggest any further refinements before we finalize our plan?
``` 
~~~~~~~~~~~
