PhD Student:  ```DIALOGUE
Hi, thanks for the thoughtful synthesis. I’ve been reflecting further and believe we could refine our approach to push the boundaries even more. Here’s an alternate plan:

1. For text generation, let’s build a π-learner MDM (170M–1.1B parameters) that entirely avoids explicit time embeddings by using an auxiliary “context evaluator” network. This network will dynamically assess the difficulty of predicting each masked token based on its contextual neighborhood. By doing so, our model will generate a “difficulty heatmap” on the fly, guiding our adaptive oracle. Alongside the traditional Top-K and margin-based oracles, we could implement an ensemble-based Monte Carlo oracle that evaluates multiple candidate orders with injected Gumbel noise—this ensemble could yield a robust fit over diverse text structures. Our performance metrics would include validation loss, generative perplexity (using LLaMA2-7B as a benchmark evaluator), and a detailed analysis of token-level entropy and diversity.

2. In our synthetic setting, let’s design a variant of the L&O-NAE-SAT task with added long-distance dependencies meant to expose imbalances in subproblem difficulty. By training a smaller MDM (6–19M parameters), we can directly log subproblem error metrics at each step. I propose experimenting with a dual-oracle system that simultaneously runs a deterministic Top-K margin oracle alongside our ensemble Monte Carlo oracle, comparing how each resolves complex dependency chains. This should reveal insights into whether stochastic exploration can better mitigate compound errors in intricate settings.

3. For the logic puzzles study, I suggest broadening the scope beyond Sudoku and Zebra. We can incorporate a grid-based puzzle mimicking elements of mini-crosswords or even incorporate visual tokens resembling aspects of simple image puzzles. Using an MDM of similar scale (6–19M parameters), we would record the evolution of puzzle accuracy with each unmasked token. In addition to evaluating our adaptive inference gains, we’d contrast these results with a baseline autoregressive model (with variants trained using both teacher-forced and free-form orders) to quantify the explicit benefit of adaptive ordering.

In summary, our experiments target: (a) context-driven difficulty estimation in text generation via a dedicated auxiliary module, (b) a synthetic, dependency-rich problem to dissect oracle performance, and (c) diverse logic puzzles to showcase adaptive ordering’s real-world accuracy gains. We'll ensure reproducibility through fixed random seeds, detailed sampling path logs (including per-step logits), and conduct ablations on oracle types, K values, noise configurations, reverse step counts, and positional encoding methods (learned versus RoPE). 

How does this revised plan sound? I’m excited to hear your thoughts on the ensemble Oracle approach and added diversity in our puzzle benchmarks!
``` 
