Postdoc:  ```PLAN
Final Plan for Adaptive Token-Ordering in MDMs versus ARMs:

Overview:
We will design a series of experiments to investigate adaptive token-ordering in masked diffusion models (MDMs) compared to autoregressive models (ARMs). Our experiments will intentionally highlight two factors: (a) training-time hardness in order-agnostic MDMs versus order-aware ARMs, and (b) inference-time gains enabled by adaptive token decoding orders. Our plan spans three families of experiments: text generation, synthetic dependency-rich tasks, and logic puzzles.

Experiment 1: Context-Aware Text Generation
• Model: Develop a π-learner masked diffusion model (MDM) ranging from 170M to 1.1B parameters. The model will eliminate explicit time embeddings and instead incorporate an auxiliary “context evaluator” network. This module dynamically computes a “difficulty heatmap” for each masked token by analyzing the surrounding context.
• Adaptive Oracle: During inference, use an ensemble approach. Combine:
  - A deterministic Top-K probability oracle.
  - A Top-K margin oracle.
  - An ensemble Monte Carlo oracle that generates and compares several candidate token orders with controlled Gumbel noise.
• Metrics: Evaluate using validation loss and generative perplexity, with LLaMA2-7B as an external evaluator. Also log token-level entropy and diversity distributions.
• Ablations: Vary oracle types, K values, noise parameters, number of reverse steps, and compare different positional encodings (learned vs. RoPE). Track how the learned token permutation diverges from a natural left-to-right order.

Experiment 2: Synthetic Dependency-Rich L&O-NAE-SAT Task
• Task Design: Create a variant of the L&O-NAE-SAT task that embeds long-distance dependencies, aiming to expose subproblem imbalances.
• Model: Train a smaller MDM (6–19M parameters) on this synthetic dataset.
• Dual-Oracle Framework: Simultaneously run two oracle strategies:
  - The conventional Top-K margin oracle.
  - The ensemble Monte Carlo oracle.
• Logging and Measurement: Record detailed subproblem error metrics at every inference step. Assess how each oracle type manages complex interdependent token relationships.
• Ablations: Experiment with different oracle configurations and noise levels to determine which best mitigates compound errors.

Experiment 3: Diverse Logic Puzzle Benchmarking
• Tasks: 
  - Classic puzzles such as Sudoku and Zebra.
  - A newly designed grid-based puzzle (resembling mini-crosswords or simple image-based puzzles) rendered as a token sequence.
• Model: Utilize MDMs scaled to 6–19M parameters. As a control, train baseline ARMs (including variants with and without teacher-forced order supervision).
• Inference: Apply the same adaptive token ordering strategies (Top-K, margin, Monte Carlo ensemble) and log per-step progress of puzzle accuracy.
• Metrics: Primary evaluation will be puzzle solving accuracy, with additional logging of the adaptive ordering progression (i.e., improvement of subproblem accuracies across unmasking steps). Compare results against ARMs to highlight the benefits of adaptive adaptive ordering in handling structural complexity.

General Experimental Details:
• Reproducibility: Fix all random seeds, run experiments across at least three seeds, and record complete sampling paths along with per-step logits.
• Comprehensive Ablations: Investigate the effects of varying oracle types, batch sizes of positions unmasked per step, noise parameters (temperature/Gumbel variants), number of reverse steps, and positional encodings (learned vs. RoPE). Analyze the closeness of the generated token order to the natural (identity) sequence, particularly from the π-learner perspective.
• Evaluation Metrics: 
  - For text: validation loss, generative perplexity, entropy, and diversity scores.
  - For synthetic tasks: subproblem-level error rates.
  - For puzzles: overall puzzle accuracy and per-step improvement logs.

This plan provides a clear, innovative strategy to study adaptive token ordering in MDMs across varied benchmarks while directly comparing against ARMs. We aim to uncover the effectiveness of context-driven difficulty metrics and ensemble-based stochastic oracles in producing more accurate and robust generative models.

Happy to discuss further adjustments if needed.
  
— End of Plan —
  
Submit this plan as our roadmap for the research.
  
 
