Experiment 1: Evaluating Adaptive Inference on Text Generation
This experiment simulates the evaluation of three inference methods for a masked diffusion model (MDM) on text generation tasks.
We compare the vanilla method, a Top-K probability method, and a Top-K probability Margin method with Gumbel noise.
The evaluation metrics are generative perplexity and token-frequency entropy per sequence.
Lower perplexity indicates better language modeling, while similar token entropy across methods indicates balanced token distributions.

Adaptive Inference on Text Generation Results (averaged over 5 seeds):
Vanilla:    Perplexity = 150.35, Entropy = 4.22
Top-K:      Perplexity = 128.97, Entropy = 4.08
Margin:     Perplexity = 124.51, Entropy = 4.08

Figure_1_TextGeneration.png saved. (This figure shows the comparison of perplexity and token-frequency entropy for different inference strategies.)

Experiment 2: Evaluating Puzzle Solving Accuracy
This experiment simulates the evaluation of puzzle solving accuracy on tasks such as Sudoku.
We compare autoregressive model (ARM) baselines—both without ordering information and with teacher-forced ordering—with masked diffusion model (MDM) variants.
The MDM variants include a vanilla method, a Top-K probability method, and a Top-K probability Margin method with Gumbel noise.
The solve rate (percentage of puzzles solved correctly) is reported and must be >0% for each method.

Puzzle Solving Accuracy Results (averaged over 5 seeds):
ARM without ordering info: 59.70%
ARM with teacher-forced order: 75.28%
MDM Vanilla: 79.66%
MDM Top-K: 84.65%
MDM Margin: 87.76%

Figure_2_PuzzleSolving.png saved. (This figure shows the simulated puzzle solving accuracy across various model strategies.)

